## 1. Introduction
Brewer’s spent grain (BSG) is the main by-product of brewing industry. Only in Europe, is produced in million of tonnes per year. This by-product is available at low or no costs and is usually disposed directly to soil or used in a landfill or as animal feed. However, due to its composition, rich in protein $20\%$ on a dry weight basis) and fibre $(70\%)$ , other applications have been proposed including the recovery of the bioactive compounds present (Mussatto, Dragone, & Roberto, 2006).
Arabinoxylans (AX) are non-starch polysaccharides composed of $\upbeta$ -D-(1,4)-linked-xylopyranosyl residues to which $\upalpha$ -Larabinofuranose units are linked as side chains and arabinose can be esterified with monomeric or dimeric ferulic acids (Saeed, Pasha, Anjum, & Sultan, 2011). They account for around $28\%$ of the BSG fibre and its consumption have been related with several health benefits (Mussatto et al., 2006; Saeed et al., 2011). AX are attached to cellulosic microfibrils by hydrogen bonding which are in turn cross-linked via diferulic acid bridges to neighbouring AX (Mandalari et al., 2005). The use of enzymes is not completely effective on the AX extraction because they cannot break most of the hydrogen bonding between cellulose and AX, thus alkaline extraction is still nowadays the most effective strategy to obtain the AX.
Components of BSG can be fractionated effectively by simple milling and sieving, and the fractionation profile will depend on the instrumentation, mill and sieve mesh sizes used (Jay et al., 2008). However, as the fractionation impact on AX alkaline extraction was not studied yet, this work aims to evaluate the BSG particle size influence on the alkaline extraction of AX.
## 2. Material and methods
## 2.1. Chemicals
All chemicals were purchased from Sigma–Aldrich (Wicklow, Ireland) except for, sulphuric acid, acetone, dichloromethane and ethanol, which were purchased from Fisher Scientific (Ballycoolin, Ireland). All chemicals were of analytical grade.
## 2.2. Brewer’s spent grain (BSG)
BSG was obtained from the micro distillery plant located in University College Cork, Cork (Ireland). The dried BSG was coarsely ground to $250\upmu\mathrm{m}$ particle size using a Perten Laboratory Mill 3100 (Perten instruments, Hägersten, Swedwen). The milled BSG was sieved by using a sieve shaker RETSCH VS1000 (Retsch GmbH,
Haan, Germany) and was passed through sieves of different mesh sizes (250, 180, 125, 90 and $63\upmu\mathrm{m}\cdot$ resulting in a separation of different particle sizes (PS): $\mathsf{P S}\le63\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (PSA); $63\upmu\mathrm{m}<\mathsf{P S}\leq90\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (PSB); $90\upmu\mathrm{m}<\mathrm{PS}\leq125\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (PSC); $125\upmu\mathrm{m}<\mathrm{PS}\leq180\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (PSD); $180\upmu\mathrm{m}<\mathrm{PS}\leq250\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (PSE) and $\mathtt{P S}>250\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (PSF). The resulting lots of grain sizes were stored in polyethylene bags vacuum packed for further analysis.
## 2.3. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM)
SEM images of the different grain sizes of BSG were acquired using a field emission gun-SEM Hitachi SU70 microscope operated at $4\mathrm{kV}.$ . Samples were deposited on a sample holder and coated with gold/palladium.
## 2.4. Arabinoxylans (AX) extraction
BSG samples were extracted with KOH 3 M, 20 mM $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ in an incubator (Innova 42, Mason technology, Dublin, Ireland) for $^{7\mathrm{h}}$ at $45^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (Reis, Coelho, Coimbra, & Abu-Ghannam, 2015). After the treatment, the suspensions were neutralised with HCl until $\mathsf{p H6-7}$ and centrifuged at $14{,}400\mathrm{rpm}$ for $20\mathrm{min}$ . The solubilised AX in the supernatants were precipitated with 5 volumes of $96\%$ ethanol, and the precipitates were isolated after centrifugation at 14,400 rpm for $10\mathrm{{min}}$ , solubilised in distilled water and dialysed with a cellulose membrane of $12\mathrm{kDa}$ cut off (Sigma, D9652). After dialysis the solutions were frozen at $-70^{\circ}C$ and finally freeze-dried.
## 2.5. Sugar analysis
Neutral sugars were released by Saeman hydrolysis and analysed as their alditol acetates by gas chromatography, and uronic acids (UA) were determined colorimetrically according to a modification of the method of Blumenkrantz and Asboe-Hansen (1973) (Coimbra, Delgadillo, Waldron, & Selvendran, 1996; Selvendran, March, & Ring, 1979). The hydrolysis of all samples was done in duplicate and each sample was injected twice.
## 2.6. Methylation analysis
Glycosidic-linkage composition was determined by gas chromatography-quadrupole mass spectrometry (GC-qMS) of
the partially methylated alditol acetates (Ciucanu & Kerek, 1984; Coelho, Rocha, & Coimbra, 2011; Harris, Henry, Blakeney, & Stone, 1984).
## 3. Results and discussion
In this work six BSG fractions were separated after milling and sieving. The BSG fractions were divided into: particles higher than $250\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Fig. 1a–d), between 250 and $180\upmu\mathrm{{m}}$ (Fig. 1e–h), $180\mathrm{-}125\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Fig. 1i–l), $125\mathrm{-}90\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Fig. $1\mathsf{m-p}$ ), $90{-}63\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Fig. 1q–t), and particles lower than $63\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Fig. $\bf{1u\mathrm{-}y\mathrm{),}}$ . The major fraction retained $(25\%)$ was between 180 and $125\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (PSD), followed by the two fractions of particle sizes $250\mathrm{-}180\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (PSE) and $90{-}63\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (PSB), weighting both $20\%$ (Table 1). Particles between 125 and $90\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (PSC) retained $17\%$ of the material. Particles higher than $250\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (PSF) comprised a fraction with $10\%$ of the material and $8\%$ consisted of particles lower than $63\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (PSA).
An increase on total sugars was observed up to grain sizes between 125 and $90\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , followed by a decrease in the fractions with lowest sizes (Table 1). This result is in accordance with the results reported by Jay et al. (2008). Total sugars were influenced mostly by the contents of xylose, arabinose and glucose, thus reflecting the presence of arabinoxylans (AX) and also the presence of starch and possibly glucans as suggested by Jay et al. (2008). The Ara/Xyl ratio observed along the grain fractions suggests the presence of AX with different degrees of branching. The ratio increases with the decrease of the particle size, suggesting more branched polymers in the fractions with small particle sizes, which is in accordance with other works reported (Jay et al., 2008).
The AX recovered from each fraction showed different sugars composition (Table 1). For particles greater than $250\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , $93\%$ of the BSG arabinose and $67\%$ of the BSG xylose were recovered. Xylose recoveries fewer than arabinose recoveries can be explained by the cell wall model reported for cereal crops: the main AX are attached by hydrogen bonding and cross linked via diferulic acid bridges to neighbouring AX. These neighbouring AX are easy to break, whereas the AX hydrogen-bonding to cellulose, essentially non substituted AX, are very difficult to break (Mandalari et al., 2005).
With the decreasing of particle size the recovery of arabinose and xylose decreased substantially reaching for particles lower than $63\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , a $37\%$ recovery of arabinose and $33\%$ recovery of xylose. Particle size between 125 and $90\upmu\mathrm{m}$ was the exception because this fraction showed the minimum recovery of arabinose and xylose, 35 and $29\%$ respectively.
The presence of AX can be inferred by 4-Xylp corresponding to the AX backbone, and 2,4-Xylp, 3,4-Xylp and 2,3,4-Xylp which are linkage points of the terminal arabinofuranose residues (Table 2). The determination of the ratios $\sf X y l_{\mathrm{total}}/t{-}\sf X y l$ and $\mathrm{Xyl_{ram}/X y l_{t o t a l}}$ allow to estimate, respectively, the degree of polymerisation (DP) and the degree of branching (DB) of the AX. The ratio $\mathrm{\Xyl_{\mathrm{total}}/t-}$ Xyl was higher in the fractions with highest particle sizes (PSE and PSF) than for the fractions with lowest particle sizes, allowing to infer that from the coarse particles can be obtained AX with higher DPs than from the small size particles. Through the different fractions, the ratio $\mathrm{Xyl_{ram}/X y l_{t o t a l}}$ in contrast increased while particle size fractions decreased.
The xylose residue O-2 monosubstituted maintained its molar percentage in all particle size fractions, with the exception of PSC. The xylose residue O-2,3 disubstituted increased among the decreased particle sizes, being the major branched identified on the extracted AX from particle sizes less than $63\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (PSA). The xylose residue O-3 monosubstituted was always the less present and reached its maximum on PSB.
These results suggest that milling of BSG acts as AX preextraction treatment in which the smaller are the particle sizes smaller and more branched polymers are. SEM images showed that for the highest particle sizes, cells are still intact (Fig. 1c, g and k) while for the smallest particles, disruption of cells have occurred (Fig. 1o, s and x) clearly observed in the highest magnification applied (Fig. 1p, t and y). According to the distribution model proposed by Viëtor (1992), AX substitution follows a pattern in which two types of sequences are included; a major one of isolated unsubstituted xylose residues separated by one or two substituted residues, and another sequence of 2 or more unsubstituted xylose residues (Viëtor, 1992). The largest particles include biggest polymers and thus the unsubstituted xylose sequences are more frequent and consequently the degree of branching decreases. Whereas in the smallest particles, smallest polymers are present which leads to more quantity of substituted structures and consequently the degree of branching increases. The exceptional results of particle sizes between 125 and $90\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (PSC) can be explained if in this fraction the quantity of the unsubstituted xylose residues sequence is greater than in the following lowest particle size fractions (PSA and PSB).
However, when particles start to be lower than $180\upmu\mathrm{{m}}$ , there is loss of the smallest polymers probably being soluble in ethanol or lost in the dialysis step, reflected in the decrease of arabinose and xylose recovery (Table 1) and the constant $\mathsf{X y l_{t o t a l}/t-X y l}$ ratio obtained in the lowest fractions (Table 2). This fact also explains the results obtained for the PSC fraction where it is probable that the smallest polymers were extracted and solubilised in ethanol or flowed throughout the dialysis membrane. Also, the increase in recoveries in the fractions lower than $90\upmu\mathrm{m}$ can be explained by the presence of more branched structures, shown by the increase of the molar $\%$ of 2,4-Xylp, 3,4-Xylp and 2,3,4-Xylp linkages, especially the disubstituted xylose residues (2,3,4-Xylp), and also by the increase of the $\mathrm{Xyl_{ram}/X y l_{t o t a l}}$ ratio calculated (Table 2). These structures were probably retained in the dialysis tube due to the highest hydrodynamic volume of the branched polymer.
The presence of 5-Araf corresponding to the feruoylated linkages of the AX (Coelho, Rocha, Saraiva, & Coimbra, 2014; Grootaert et al., 2007; Jay et al., 2008), is in accordance with the type of AX extracted; less branched AX presented less content of feruoylated linkages.
The 2-Araf linkage was also identified and this linkage is usually from the arabinan side chain of arabinogalactans (AG) (Saeed et al., 2011). Moreover, 3-Galp linkage was found in residual amounts which correspond to the typical AG backbone, simultaneously with the substituted galactose residues 6-Galp and 3,6-Galp, all accounting for the presence of residual AG. The presence of these polysaccharides was also reported in barley and barley malt (Dervilly et al., 2002). Residual AG were extracted from PSD and the amount of AG extracted increased when particle sizes decreased. Jay et al. (2008) reported that protein content increased by decreasing particle sizes and as AG are usually linked with peptides (Saeed et al., 2011), the AG content increased by decreasing particle sizes is in accordance with the findings previously reported.
The presence of 4-Glcp and 4,6-Glcp linkages suggests the presence of starch in similar residual amounts in all fractions $(8-10\%)$ . It was reported that a considerable increase in starch is associated with a decrease of particle size grain fractions (Jay et al., 2008), which means that starch is also being lost during the extraction process of this study. The 3-Glcp is diagnostic of $\beta$ - glucans, and is present in residual amounts in all particle size fractions. The presence of 2-Manp, 6-Manp and 2,6-Manp, suggest the presence of mannoproteins from yeasts (Lesage & Bussey, 2006; Lipke & Ovalle, 1998), reflecting possible contamination of the BSG analysed being extracted in particle sizes lower than $125\upmu\mathrm{m}$ .
## 4. Conclusion
The milling and sieving of BSG acts as an assisted extraction of AX since each grain fraction may be selected in order to obtain different AX structures of distinct polymerisation and branching degrees. Alkaline extraction of BSG particle sizes higher than $180\upmu\mathrm{{m}}$ lead to AX rich extracts with an estimate DP average of 24 and an estimate DB of 0.2 while lower than $180\upmu\mathrm{{m}}$ lead to AX rich extracts with an estimate DP average between 7 and 12 and an estimate DB between 0.4 and 0.5. These extracts obtained from the different grain fractions should be further studied for their functional properties since it is well known that DP and DB strongly influence the functional properties of AX. Also, the presence of residual AG should be evaluated for the improvement of the functional properties of the extracts.
